WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:03.988
This is ForeCheck.

2
00:00:03.888 --> 00:00:08.946
Your whole training plan, from the season down to the very last set, in one place.

3
00:00:08.846 --> 00:00:12.150
Let me show you how fast planning in the gym becomes!

4
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:14.791
It all starts with the season.

5
00:00:14.691 --> 00:00:21.323
ForeCheck reads your game calendar and rates every single week: heavy, build, normal, or deload.

6
00:00:21.223 --> 00:00:26.630
Games are the load, so the plan follows the schedule. Not the other way round.

7
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.125
Create a mesocycle in seconds.

8
00:00:29.025 --> 00:00:36.012
Pick a preset, like three loading weeks and a deload, choose the training phase, and every week gets its job.

9
00:00:35.912 --> 00:00:42.140
Each preset shows its source, so you always know what is evidence, and what is coaching practice.

10
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.671
Now, the gym.

11
00:00:43.571 --> 00:00:48.329
The Strength Room puts your lifting week right next to the games.

12
00:00:48.229 --> 00:00:55.780
Game day, the day before, the day after. You see at a glance where strength fits, and where it does not.

13
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.747
Build a workout with blocks, supersets and circuits.

14
00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:07.130
Pick a phase, and new lines fill themselves: sets, reps, intensity and rest.

15
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.490
Prefer reps in reserve? Just type it in.

16
00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.568
And here is the magic.

17
00:01:12.468 --> 00:01:17.337
ForeCheck turns every prescription into kilos, for every single player.

18
00:01:17.237 --> 00:01:22.509
From a tested max, from the sets he logged, or from a careful starting guess.

19
00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:27.950
Always with a confidence label, so you know exactly what each number is built on.

20
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:33.607
Maxes are labelled: tested, training max, heavy singles, or estimated.

21
00:01:33.507 --> 00:01:37.220
Anything older than six weeks is flagged as stale.

22
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.060
Log what was really lifted, and the next suggestion learns from it.

23
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.553
In season, ForeCheck watches the gaps.

24
00:01:45.453 --> 00:01:51.110
Who has not lifted for a week? Who is overdue? One look. No spreadsheets.

25
00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.799
Now, brand new: the deload week.

26
00:01:53.699 --> 00:01:59.738
When a week is a deload, ForeCheck drafts it for you, straight from your last loading week.

27
00:01:59.638 --> 00:02:06.200
Fewer sets, easier effort, or lighter loads. Add a lower strain variant, or more rest, if you like.

28
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.207
Every change is shown line by line, with the reason why.

29
00:02:10.107 --> 00:02:14.588
Sessions only land on days that have something planned in your calendar.

30
00:02:14.488 --> 00:02:19.700
And if the week is still empty, ForeCheck asks: do you want me to use this schedule?

31
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.890
Drag a day across, and you are done.

32
00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.635
After the game, it gets personal.

33
00:02:24.535 --> 00:02:31.395
In a deload week, after game lifting is suggested only for players who did not play, or had low minutes.

34
00:02:31.295 --> 00:02:36.725
It uses league minutes and match load, and it always tells you which data it used.

35
00:02:36.625 --> 00:02:39.780
Nothing changes on its own. You stay in charge.

36
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:46.050
Body composition and nutrition guides sit right next to it, and different methods are never mixed in one trend.

37
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.604
Individual plans, made easy.

38
00:02:48.504 --> 00:02:54.258
Because the players who did not play deserve a plan as smart as the ones who did.

39
00:02:54.158 --> 00:02:56.910
ForeCheck. Plan smarter. Train better.
